/-!
# $q$-expansion of the pointwise prime Hecke operator $T_p$ (with $U_p$ when $p\mid N$)

Theorem statement: `MTT.hasSum_heckePrime` (`a5992056-0712-4c6f-95c5-54e020c1da6c`), by cbirkbeck,
2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `4296bb05-1348-49dd-a741-c50e60c08620`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $f:\mathfrak H\to\mathbf C$ be a function admitting a convergent $q$-expansion of period one:
for every $\sigma\in\mathfrak H$,
$$f(\sigma)=\sum_{n\ge0}a_n\,q_\sigma^{\,n},\qquad q_\sigma=e^{2\pi i\sigma},$$
the series converging (unconditionally) at every point. Fix a prime $p$, a weight $k$ and a scalar
$e\in\mathbf C$; in applications $e=\varepsilon(p)$ is the value of a nebentypus, so $e=0$ when
$p\mid N$. The pointwise prime Hecke operator of the mission is
$$(T_pf)(\tau)=\frac1p\sum_{b=0}^{p-1}f\Big(\frac{\tau+b}{p}\Big)+e\,p^{k-1}f(p\tau).$$
**Claim.** For every $\tau\in\mathfrak H$,
$$(T_pf)(\tau)=\sum_{n\ge0}\Big(a_{pn}+e\,p^{k-1}\,a_{n/p}\Big)q_\tau^{\,n},\qquad a_{n/p}:=0\
\text{unless } p\mid n,$$
the series again converging unconditionally at every $\tau$.

This is the classical formula for the Fourier coefficients of $T_pf$ (Diamond–Shurman, Proposition
5.3.1), stated here for an arbitrary function with a convergent $q$-series: no modularity is used.
The first term is the $U_p$ sieve, obtained from $q_{(\tau+b)/p}=q_{\tau/p}\,\zeta_p^{\,b}$ and the
orthogonality relation $\sum_{b=0}^{p-1}\zeta_p^{\,nb}=p\,[p\mid n]$ for the primitive $p$-th root
of unity $\zeta_p=e^{2\pi i/p}$; the second term is the reindexing $q_{p\tau}=q_\tau^{\,p}$. In
Lean, $a_{n/p}$ is `if p ∣ n then a (n / p) else 0` with natural-number division, and convergence is
`HasSum`.

## Explanation of the source proof

We prove the coefficient formula for the pointwise prime Hecke operator
$$(T_pf)(\tau)=\frac1p\sum_{b=0}^{p-1}f\Big(\frac{\tau+b}{p}\Big)+e\,p^{k-1}f(p\tau)$$
directly from the hypothesis that $f(\sigma)=\sum_{n\ge0}a_nq_\sigma^{\,n}$ converges at every
$\sigma\in\mathfrak H$, where $q_\sigma=e^{2\pi i\sigma}$. The two summands are treated separately
and then added (`HasSum.add`).

**The $U_p$ term.** Put $w=q_{\tau/p}=e^{2\pi i\tau/p}$ and $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/p}$, so that
$w^p=q_\tau$ and $q_{(\tau+b)/p}=w\,\zeta^{\,b}$ (`qParam_add`, `qParam_mul_nat`). Since
$\operatorname{Im}\frac{\tau+b}{p}>0$, the points $\sigma_b=\frac{\tau+b}{p}$ lie in $\mathfrak H$
and `ofComplex` returns them unchanged (`ofComplex_apply_of_im_pos`). Summing the expansions of
$f(\sigma_b)$ over $b$ (`hasSum_sum`) and exchanging the finite sum with the series gives
$$\sum_{b=0}^{p-1}f(\sigma_b)=\sum_{n\ge0}a_n\,w^n\sum_{b=0}^{p-1}\zeta^{\,nb}.$$
The inner sum is the orthogonality relation $\sum_{b<p}\zeta^{nb}=p\,[p\mid n]$
(`sum_qParam_pow_period_one`): $\zeta$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity
(`Complex.isPrimitiveRoot_exp`), so $\zeta^n=1$ when $p\mid n$, while for $p\nmid n$ the geometric
sum $\sum_b(\zeta^n)^b=\frac{(\zeta^n)^p-1}{\zeta^n-1}$ vanishes (`geom_sum_eq`, with $\zeta^n\neq1$
from `IsPrimitiveRoot.pow_eq_one_iff_dvd`). Hence $\frac1p\sum_bf(\sigma_b)=\sum_{p\mid n}a_nw^n$.
Reindexing along the injection $m\mapsto pm$ (`Function.Injective.hasSum_iff`, the terms off its
range being zero) and using $w^{pm}=q_\tau^{\,m}$ yields
$$\frac1p\sum_{b=0}^{p-1}f\Big(\frac{\tau+b}{p}\Big)=\sum_{m\ge0}a_{pm}\,q_\tau^{\,m}\qquad(\text{`hasSum_heckePrime_upper`}).$$

**The $V_p$ term.** Since $\operatorname{Im}(p\tau)>0$,
$f(p\tau)=\sum_na_nq_{p\tau}^{\,n}=\sum_na_nq_\tau^{\,pn}$ by $q_{p\tau}=q_\tau^{\,p}$. Reindexing
along $m\mapsto pm$ in the other direction (again `Function.Injective.hasSum_iff`) gives
$$f(p\tau)=\sum_{m\ge0}[p\mid m]\,a_{m/p}\,q_\tau^{\,m}\qquad(\text{`hasSum_heckePrime_lower`}),$$
with $[p\mid m]a_{m/p}$ meaning `if p ∣ m then a (m / p) else 0`.

**Assembly.** Multiplying the second series by $e\,p^{k-1}$ (`HasSum.const_smul`) and adding the two
gives a series with coefficients $a_{pm}+e\,p^{k-1}[p\mid m]a_{m/p}$ converging to
$\frac1p\sum_bf(\frac{\tau+b}{p})+e\,p^{k-1}f(p\tau)$, which is $(T_pf)(\tau)$ by definition of
`MTT.heckePrime`; the coefficient identity is checked by `split_ifs <;> ring`.

This is Diamond–Shurman, Proposition 5.3.1, stripped of modularity: only the convergent $q$-series
is used, so the result applies verbatim to the $U_p$ case $e=0$.

